1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,568
The school asks for more than separate definitions. You must move
through an encounter, showing how each observation changes the next
decision. This session connects the Week One teaching into two
original simulation variants.

2
00:00:14,568 --> 00:00:29,448
The first variant practises the approach to a prone unresponsive
person. The second combines a fall with an underlying illness and
hidden deterioration. They rehearse school task types without
reproducing a school assessment answer.

3
00:00:29,448 --> 00:00:43,104
Use a trainer and an observer for physical tasks. The observer gives
only information actually available at each stage; the team must
request or obtain the rest instead of assuming a normal examination.

4
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:55,704
The goal is a complete reasoning and action chain, not speed without
care. The school timed rotation is a practice structure, not a
guarantee of competence or permission for unsupervised treatment.

5
00:00:55,704 --> 00:01:06,792
At the start, identify a lead and assign the immediate work. A task
such as check breathing and tell me rate, depth and adequacy is
clearer than somebody do vitals.

6
00:01:06,792 --> 00:01:18,384
Use closed-loop communication: a named person acknowledges, performs
and reports the result. The lead confirms that the result has been
heard and changes the plan when necessary.

7
00:01:18,384 --> 00:01:30,048
Roles can change as the patient changes. If two providers are needed
for effective ventilation, stop nonessential recording or equipment
tidying and redirect the available hands.

8
00:01:30,048 --> 00:01:42,552
The recorder documents what actually happened, including delay or
uncertainty. A neat record assembled from guessed values is less
useful than an honest chronology of a difficult simulation.

9
00:01:42,552 --> 00:01:54,072
In this original variant, an adult is found face down beside a bicycle
path. Control the moving-traffic risk and preserve access before
committing the team beside the person.

10
00:01:54,072 --> 00:02:07,416
Ask bystanders what they directly saw, when the person was last known
responsive and whether a collision or collapse preceded the position.
Attribute those reports rather than converting them into confirmed
findings.

11
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:20,736
Check responsiveness and obvious immediately accessible threats while
planning access to the airway and chest. A rigid recital of a scene
checklist must not obscure an urgent functional problem.

12
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:32,976
Unknown cause keeps both injury and illness in view. Spinal concern
changes handling, but does not justify leaving an inaccessible,
threatened airway unassessed indefinitely.

13
00:02:32,976 --> 00:02:45,696
The selected Nancy airway page directs a prone unresponsive patient to
be positioned for airway and breathing assessment, using a unit log
roll. It does not make a blocked airway safer to leave face down.

14
00:02:45,696 --> 00:03:00,792
Plan with the actual team and trainer. State who coordinates, who
controls the relevant body regions and where the patient will end up.
Prepare suction and ventilation equipment before a move likely to
reveal an airway problem.

15
00:03:00,792 --> 00:03:13,704
The principle is coordinated movement as a unit, with spinal motion
minimized when indicated. Do not twist the head independently of the
trunk or force an injured body into a position against resistance.

16
00:03:13,704 --> 00:03:25,608
Your instructor must demonstrate the actual personnel arrangement and
approved technique. Practise with a suitable manikin rather than an
unsupported improvised roll on a real person.

17
00:03:25,608 --> 00:03:36,480
Repositioning is not the endpoint. Immediately inspect and open or
clear the airway as needed, assess actual breathing and circulation,
and act on the findings.

18
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:51,504
A patient who is not breathing normally needs the appropriate
resuscitation branch. A patient who is breathing adequately but cannot
protect the airway needs protection and close monitoring under the
actual injury and positioning context.

19
00:03:51,504 --> 00:04:02,976
Do not call the roll successful just because the person is supine.
Check whether the airway became obstructed, support detached, bleeding
changed or an injury became more apparent.

20
00:04:02,976 --> 00:04:14,448
State the result aloud and record the major move and subsequent
reassessment. This makes the handoff more useful and gives the
observer evidence of a safe action-response loop.

21
00:04:14,448 --> 00:04:29,400
Name and perform the school primary-survey tasks, but treat the
immediate life threat when found. Current A two explicitly prioritizes
catastrophic bleeding and coordinates unconscious breathing and
circulation assessment.

22
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:41,400
Rapid body survey looks for important concealed threats without
turning the first minutes into a routine complete examination. Explain
exposure, maintain warmth and protect dignity.

23
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:55,512
SOAPPP is the school stabilization reminder; the saved BCPEG
intervention wording is different. In either source, select indicated
care, not automatic oxygen or an adjunct for every patient.

24
00:04:55,512 --> 00:05:09,120
The useful verbalization is finding, meaning, action and reassessment.
Saying airway clear while never examining it, or calling breathing
adequate from a saturation alone, does not show the skill.

25
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:23,112
After identifying and addressing immediate threats, state your
transport category and the findings supporting it. The BCPEG generic
flow permits earlier conveyance when condition and definitive-care
needs require it.

26
00:05:23,112 --> 00:05:34,632
Distinguish an action that changes immediate survival from a routine
task that can occur en route. A school scene-time benchmark is not
permission to use every remaining minute before leaving.

27
00:05:34,632 --> 00:05:47,712
Movement needs an equipment and staffing plan, especially if airway
support must continue. Check the route, straps, device security and
access to the patient before committing to a narrow space.

28
00:05:47,712 --> 00:06:03,168
Use an early report to request appropriate resources and prepare the
receiver. Exact destination and clinical pathway depend on the actual
presentation and service system; urgency does not justify an invented
local rule.

29
00:06:03,168 --> 00:06:14,688
The second original simulation uses a seventy-seven-year-old man with
hip pain after a fall. Family report that he is normally independent
but is unusually confused today.

30
00:06:14,688 --> 00:06:27,720
Do not close the case as a simple mechanical fall. Ask whether
illness, faintness or another problem caused the event, and examine
both the possible injury and the current physiological disturbance.

31
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:43,152
Anticoagulant use and uncertain head impact are important reported
risks, not proof of a brain bleed. Attribute the history, seek missing
details and communicate uncertainty rather than making a false
definitive diagnosis.

32
00:06:43,152 --> 00:06:57,672
New confusion changes urgency independently of the pain complaint.
Establish the previous baseline and timeline, check glucose and
complete the immediate functional assessment before an extended
orthopedic history.

33
00:06:57,672 --> 00:07:10,368
Ask a question because its answer can change the plan. Medication
history should establish actual names, use, timing and relevant doses,
not simply that the patient takes pills.

34
00:07:10,368 --> 00:07:26,424
For pain, obtain location, radiation, quality, onset and provoking
factors, plus a patient-reported severity and trend. Do not
deliberately repeat painful movement to reproduce a finding that is
already clear.

35
00:07:26,424 --> 00:07:39,336
For the fall, establish the mechanism, possible loss of consciousness
and subsequent movement. Ask what happened before the fall; an illness
causing collapse may be the more time-critical problem.

36
00:07:39,336 --> 00:07:54,240
Include functional inquiry: what could the person do yesterday and
what can they do now? If confusion limits reliability, document
collateral information and unresolved questions rather than forcing a
complete-sounding history.

37
00:07:54,240 --> 00:08:07,200
The observer now provides measured values, not a diagnosis: twenty-six
breaths, saturation ninety-five on air, pulse one hundred twelve and
manual pressure one hundred two over sixty-four.

38
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:22,080
Temperature is thirty-eight point four by the stated method, and
glucose is six point eight millimoles per litre. New confusion was
established against the reported baseline, not inferred from an
unfamiliar accent or hearing problem.

39
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:34,320
Verify measurement quality and record time, method and oxygen context.
If a number conflicts with the examination, recheck it without
delaying support for an obvious clinical threat.

40
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:46,680
A single glucose within a usual range does not make the patient
stable. The complete pattern and trajectory determine concern, while
each exact number allows another clinician to check your reasoning.

41
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:57,384
For this adult example, use scale one. Twenty-six breaths scores three
and ninety-five percent scores one. On air there are no added oxygen
points.

42
00:08:57,384 --> 00:09:08,040
A systolic pressure of one hundred two scores one, pulse one hundred
twelve scores two, temperature thirty-eight point four scores one and
new confusion scores three.

43
00:09:08,040 --> 00:09:21,024
Add all six contributions to obtain eleven. Do not leave consciousness
out because a GCS is also charted, or forget oxygen points if you
later calculate a new score during supplemental oxygen.

44
00:09:21,024 --> 00:09:34,680
This high aggregate and single severe parameter support urgent
escalation, but do not diagnose the cause. You still need the clinical
examination, repeated observations and the applicable ambulance
pathway.

45
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:50,688
Fever, confusion, tachypnea and circulation changes raise concern for
infection-associated deterioration, including possible sepsis. A
temperature alone cannot establish it, and absence of fever would not
safely exclude it.

46
00:09:50,688 --> 00:10:03,888
The fall also raises injury concerns, and anticoagulation increases
the importance of an accurate history and examination. Infection and
injury are not mutually exclusive explanations.

47
00:10:03,888 --> 00:10:16,944
Do not explain every abnormality as pain or assume that one plausible
diagnosis accounts for all findings. State the urgent physiological
problem and preserve the serious alternatives in the report.

48
00:10:16,944 --> 00:10:29,976
Provide indicated support, escalate and reassess. Do not make an
unstable patient walk merely to test the hip, and do not administer a
treatment simply because a score has a particular value.

49
00:10:29,976 --> 00:10:43,632
Explain the movement and obtain the applicable cooperation or
authority. Choose the approved method according to suspected injury,
patient size, tolerance, staffing and route rather than convenience
alone.

50
00:10:43,632 --> 00:10:56,616
Coordinate the movement under one lead and minimize unnecessary
painful motion. Maintain access to any support and protect lines,
oxygen tubing and monitoring leads from entanglement.

51
00:10:56,616 --> 00:11:08,736
After the major move, repeat the relevant assessment rather than
copying the earlier numbers. If the patient worsens, stop where safe,
reassess and address the immediate problem.

52
00:11:08,736 --> 00:11:21,288
Document the movement method, assistance and response. A patient who
was stable on the floor can deteriorate during packaging, so the
reassessment is a clinical action, not a documentation formality.

53
00:11:21,288 --> 00:11:34,512
Begin with your role and the urgent concern: an older fall patient has
new confusion and abnormal physiology. Do not open with an extended
chronological story that hides the reason for calling.

54
00:11:34,512 --> 00:11:48,288
Give the relevant baseline and history, then exact observations with
time and oxygen context. State NEWS2 eleven and the components that
matter; the number should summarize evidence, not replace it.

55
00:11:48,288 --> 00:12:01,512
Report what was actually done and the response, then make the request
explicit. If a treatment or examination has not yet occurred, say so
rather than speaking in a way that implies completion.

56
00:12:01,512 --> 00:12:15,048
Confirm instructions with a read-back when appropriate and document
them. A consultation does not transfer patient responsibility by
itself; the later transfer uses the applicable handover structure and
acceptance.

57
00:12:15,048 --> 00:12:29,712
A timeline distinguishes event time from charting time. In this
fictional demonstration, contact is ten hundred and the measured
baseline is ten hundred four; do not backdate a later observation to
the initial encounter.

58
00:12:29,712 --> 00:12:45,528
Each action needs its reason and response. If oxygen is given,
document the indication, device, flow and effect. If a task is
declined or not possible, record what, why and what alternative was
used.

59
00:12:45,528 --> 00:12:58,320
Do not invent the second observation set to finish a worksheet. The
simulator or actual examination must provide the value; a blank or
explained omission is more truthful than a copied baseline.

60
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:10,296
Keep case information separate from real patient data. The
demonstration is for learning the record structure; the student
produces the actual school practice PCR from their own observed
station.

61
00:13:10,296 --> 00:13:24,600
The local BCPEG page seventeen is an image of the blank examination
PCR. Its history boxes and examination fields are separate, so
attribute reported information and keep it distinct from findings you
obtained.

62
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:39,264
Its serial table includes GCS components and vital observations. Enter
actual units and methods where needed, and record a non-testable
component or an unobtainable measurement honestly rather than
inventing a normal.

63
00:13:39,264 --> 00:13:52,704
Use the care, protocol and additional-comment areas to connect
actions, reasons and response. A tick beside oxygen does not replace
its relevant device, flow and effect information.

64
00:13:52,704 --> 00:14:08,424
Check identifiers, chronology, internal consistency and handover
details before finalizing. Corrections must preserve the original
record according to the actual documentation system; never erase an
inconvenient observation.

65
00:14:08,424 --> 00:14:19,896
Explain why each protective item is needed for the task and
anticipated exposure. Equipment selection and correct removal matter
more than wearing the greatest possible number of items.

66
00:14:19,896 --> 00:14:35,160
Use the taught current procedure, including the respirator fit and
seal requirements when relevant. The paired reading identifies the
archived checklist separately so its sequence is not silently treated
as every current local procedure.

67
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:47,688
Ask the observer to identify a contamination event such as a dirty
glove touching a clean meter. Stop, clean or replace the affected
items as required and repair the clean-dirty workflow.

68
00:14:47,688 --> 00:14:59,832
Report an exposure promptly under the applicable procedure. A perfect-
looking final glove removal does not undo contamination that already
travelled to equipment, clothing or mucosa.

69
00:14:59,832 --> 00:15:13,752
The school introduces a thirty-minute PAA rotation. Unless the
instructor supplies a more specific station schedule, use that as the
overall practice container rather than inventing mandatory minute-by-
minute scoring rules.

70
00:15:13,752 --> 00:15:23,976
Complete the case, report and a brief record outline, then debrief the
decisive branch: what did you notice, what did it mean, what did you
do and what changed?

71
00:15:23,976 --> 00:15:37,008
Have each member lead and observe across repeated rotations. Change a
meaningful feature rather than memorizing one script, such as normal
versus ineffective breathing or a stable versus falling pressure.

72
00:15:37,008 --> 00:15:50,184
Choose a specific improvement to test immediately: a clearer task
instruction, better cuff positioning or an earlier airway
reassessment. Feedback should name observed behaviour rather than
judge the person.

73
00:15:50,184 --> 00:16:07,680
The school links a short BVM demonstration in its Weekend One
practical resources. First identify the clinical branch: a ventilation
problem is not the same as isolated low oxygen saturation, and the
current trained resuscitation pathway determines the sequence.

74
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:20,088
BVM provides positive-pressure breaths when spontaneous ventilation is
absent or inadequate. It cannot compensate for an obstructed upper
airway until the passage is opened or cleared.

75
00:16:20,088 --> 00:16:34,080
Airway patency, oxygenation and ventilation are related but distinct.
A reassuring saturation does not prove adequate ventilation, and
oxygen through a mask alone does not replace a missing breath.

76
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:50,904
Do not memorize one ventilation rate for every situation. A pulse-
present respiratory-arrest branch, cardiac arrest and a procedure-
specific PEEP technique may have different instructions; identify the
patient and use the current school/service protocol.

77
00:16:50,904 --> 00:17:07,944
Use the school video to recognize the equipment and see one
demonstration, not as a substitute for checking the device in your own
lab. The assembly, oxygen reservoir and connections vary by model;
follow that manufacturer’s instructions and the instructor’s checkoff.

78
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:24,312
Before use, inspect that the mask suits the patient, the bag and one-
way valve function, the oxygen source is connected when indicated, and
tubing and reservoir are arranged for the actual model. A familiar-
looking bag is not proof that it works.

79
00:17:24,312 --> 00:17:37,128
Position yourself where the airway and chest can be seen. In a team,
name who maintains the airway and mask seal, who delivers breaths, and
who watches chest movement and reports changes.

80
00:17:37,128 --> 00:17:50,016
If secretions or vomit threaten patency, have appropriate suction
ready and use the taught technique. Protect against exposure using
current routine practices and task-specific precautions.

81
00:17:50,016 --> 00:18:10,296
BCEHS B01 identifies a patent airway and a tight mask seal as
prerequisites for effective BVM. With loss of airway tone, the jaw
thrust is an effective opening maneuver; where cervical injury is not
suspected, the current guideline describes jaw thrust and head-
tilt/chin-lift together.

82
00:18:10,296 --> 00:18:22,608
BCEHS says optimal BVM ventilation is performed by two providers. One
can maintain jaw position and the mask seal while the other delivers
breaths and watches the patient with the team.

83
00:18:22,608 --> 00:18:38,712
The school’s linked video is an older visual resource. A sampled frame
explains the E-C hand technique when an EMT is alone; that is a useful
contrast, not evidence that a lone-rescuer grip is preferable when a
second provider is available.

84
00:18:38,712 --> 00:18:51,144
Practice the hand placement on the school’s manikin using the
instructor’s chosen method. Never infer that a correctly shaped grip
guarantees an open airway, a seal or a delivered breath.

85
00:18:51,144 --> 00:19:06,696
BCEHS B01 calls for the chest to be appropriately exposed so that
ventilation effectiveness is judged by chest rise. Only a slight but
definite rise is needed for safe ventilation; more bag compression is
not automatically better.

86
00:19:06,696 --> 00:19:19,800
If the chest does not rise, stop and troubleshoot rather than forcing
larger breaths: recheck airway position, mask fit, obstruction,
equipment and the need for trained help or an adjunct.

87
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:32,544
Watch the patient’s work of breathing, color, responsiveness and
perfusion, and compare with the pre-support state. A pulse-oximeter
value may lag and does not measure ventilation directly.

88
00:19:32,544 --> 00:19:47,616
Capnography can add useful ventilation information when available and
appropriate, but it does not replace visible patient assessment or the
actual local pathway. If the patient worsens, escalate and reassess
immediately.

89
00:19:47,616 --> 00:20:01,704
The school-link viewer did not provide an exportable transcript during
review, so this lesson does not claim a complete spoken-content
summary. Use the clip for visual orientation and follow the captions
available in your own course player.

90
00:20:01,704 --> 00:20:15,720
Pause the video to identify what the operator’s hands are doing,
whether the airway remains positioned, what feedback the chest gives
and whether another helper is present. Do not copy a movement without
understanding its purpose.

91
00:20:15,720 --> 00:20:29,376
Then practise with the actual school equipment and manikin. The
observer should report visible chest rise, seal problems and changes
in the patient scenario; give one specific correction and repeat the
attempt.

92
00:20:29,376 --> 00:20:42,648
Watching or playing a video is not proof of physical competence. The
instructor’s current technique, relevant authorization, device
instructions and supervised checkoff remain the standard for
performance.

93
00:20:42,648 --> 00:20:56,928
Before the school check, explain why your priority changed in each
case, calculate a new NEWS2 and identify what the number cannot
diagnose. This tests understanding rather than recognition of familiar
words.

94
00:20:56,928 --> 00:21:09,744
Demonstrate the applicable skills under supervision: PPE, measurement,
safe coordinated repositioning and the PAA encounter. Playback
completion does not replace the instructor observation.

95
00:21:09,744 --> 00:21:23,472
The school pre-quiz requirement remains a student task; the saved
outline states at least seventy-five percent. This course neither
opens the quiz nor supplies its answers or a fabricated completion.

96
00:21:23,472 --> 00:21:37,008
Use the reading tracker to distinguish taught concepts from unresolved
assigned-edition coverage. The next week builds on these functions, so
repair a specific weak decision or technique instead of just
collecting more notes.
